1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:08,598
Hi, this is Shakhoyat, roll 2107104, from CSE 2K21.

2
00:00:08,598 --> 00:00:14,394
I am presenting the full functionality of my Computer Graphics project, with an AI voice.

3
00:00:14,394 --> 00:00:15,546
Sorry for that!

4
00:00:15,546 --> 00:00:17,606
I hope you enjoy the depth of the project.

5
00:00:18,517 --> 00:00:20,429
This is ThermalSense:

6
00:00:20,429 --> 00:00:25,501
a military outpost at night, watched by two drones that carry thermal cameras.

7
00:00:26,417 --> 00:00:32,825
When an intruder comes, the drones find it, track it, and fire so that both of their rounds arrive together.

8
00:00:33,717 --> 00:00:35,715
And none of this is a recording.

9
00:00:35,715 --> 00:00:39,285
I can climb into this tank myself, drive it, and fire.

10
00:00:40,183 --> 00:00:47,193
In this video I will first go through every topic of our graphics lab, one by one, and show each of them inside this scene.

11
00:00:47,193 --> 00:00:53,287
After that I will show the more advanced techniques, and the algorithms that let the outpost think for itself.

12
00:00:54,183 --> 00:00:55,776
One note before we start.

13
00:00:55,776 --> 00:00:58,889
A scene of this size cannot be managed from memory.

14
00:00:58,889 --> 00:01:05,041
So while I was building it, I recorded every point of every object in an Excel workbook.

15
00:01:05,041 --> 00:01:13,599
It has six thousand and sixty-nine components, each with its position in the world, and every number you will hear in this video comes from that workbook.

16
00:01:14,483 --> 00:01:19,569
Let me first show you the place, a military outpost about seventy metres across.

17
00:01:19,569 --> 00:01:23,086
A command bunker sits under the ground in the middle.

18
00:01:23,086 --> 00:01:33,419
Around it there are a helipad, a radar, an anti-aircraft gun, missile and rocket launchers, three tanks, a barracks hall, guard towers, and a fence with a single gate.

19
00:01:34,317 --> 00:01:35,812
Here it is, running.

20
00:01:35,812 --> 00:01:39,243
The camera is free, so I can fly wherever I like.

21
00:01:39,243 --> 00:01:46,461
Soldiers live and work here, two drones wait on their pads, and every lamp, door and vehicle really works.

22
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:48,919
Let me come down to the road:

23
00:01:48,919 --> 00:01:53,566
in through the gate, past the flag, and towards the ramp that leads under the ground.

24
00:01:54,450 --> 00:01:56,582
The Escape key opens a menu.

25
00:01:56,582 --> 00:02:02,930
From here I can travel to any unit, set the time of day, change the view, and adjust the settings.

26
00:02:02,930 --> 00:02:06,162
The help screen lists more than a hundred and twenty keys.

27
00:02:07,050 --> 00:02:09,182
Here is the plan of the video.

28
00:02:09,182 --> 00:02:20,028
Part one is the graphics lab, from primitives and transformations, through viewing, clipping and rasterisation, to lighting, shading, texture and ray tracing.

29
00:02:20,028 --> 00:02:26,707
Part two goes beyond the lab, to the techniques that make the picture look real and the world feel alive.

30
00:02:26,707 --> 00:02:29,709
In part three you take control of the outpost.

31
00:02:29,709 --> 00:02:38,730
And parts four and five are about its algorithms, how a drone sees and tracks, and how the drones, the intruders and the soldiers decide what to do.

32
00:02:39,617 --> 00:02:41,100
Let us begin with the lab.

33
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:44,862
Everything starts from the only shape a graphics card can really draw:

34
00:02:44,862 --> 00:02:46,097
a triangle.

35
00:02:46,983 --> 00:02:52,498
A triangle has three corners, and each corner stores a position and a normal.

36
00:02:52,498 --> 00:02:54,777
Two triangles make a square face.

37
00:02:54,777 --> 00:02:56,334
Six faces make a cube.

38
00:02:56,334 --> 00:03:03,086
The two triangles of a face share their corners, so I store each corner once and refer to it by an index:

39
00:03:03,086 --> 00:03:06,726
twenty-four corners and thirty-six indices for the whole cube.

40
00:03:06,726 --> 00:03:08,380
That is not a small saving.

41
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:14,295
With indices the full scene takes about twelve milliseconds per frame, and without them fifty-three.

42
00:03:15,183 --> 00:03:23,382
A curved shape is the same idea with more pieces, so I cut a sphere into slices, like an orange, and into stacks from pole to pole.

43
00:03:23,382 --> 00:03:25,661
With eight slices it is a rough ball.

44
00:03:25,661 --> 00:03:27,340
With sixteen it is better.

45
00:03:27,340 --> 00:03:34,999
With thirty-six slices and eighteen stacks it has one thousand two hundred and twenty-four triangles, and it finally looks round.

46
00:03:34,999 --> 00:03:39,303
Two short formulas give the number of corners and triangles for any choice.

47
00:03:40,217 --> 00:03:44,984
The whole outpost is built from only sixteen primitives like these.

48
00:03:44,984 --> 00:03:51,087
First the boxes, which are the cube, the cuboid, the wedge, the tapered box and the pyramid.

49
00:03:51,087 --> 00:03:55,536
Then the round shapes, the sphere, the hemisphere and the capsule.

50
00:03:55,536 --> 00:04:00,609
Then the shapes turned about an axis, the cylinder, the frustum and the cone.

51
00:04:00,609 --> 00:04:04,592
Then the rings, a torus, an annulus and a helix.

52
00:04:04,592 --> 00:04:09,569
And finally two special ones, a single blade of grass and the ground itself.

53
00:04:10,483 --> 00:04:16,120
Inside the program, the F key recolours every part by the primitive it is made of.

54
00:04:16,120 --> 00:04:20,618
Blue is a box, green is a cylinder, orange is a sphere.

55
00:04:20,618 --> 00:04:26,587
The camp has five thousand four hundred and seventy-four parts, and every one of them is one of the sixteen.

56
00:04:27,483 --> 00:04:33,315
The G key pulls every object apart into its primitives, and then puts it back together.

57
00:04:34,483 --> 00:04:41,275
And the X key shows the triangles themselves, first with the hidden lines removed, and then as an x-ray.

58
00:04:42,483 --> 00:04:45,534
So an object is simply a list of parts.

59
00:04:45,534 --> 00:04:48,010
This drone has one hundred and fifty-eight of them:

60
00:04:48,010 --> 00:04:55,299
first the body, then the arms, the motors and rotors, and finally the camera, the searchlight and the rounds on their rails.

61
00:04:56,183 --> 00:05:00,546
Every primitive starts one unit big, sitting at the origin.

62
00:05:00,546 --> 00:05:03,205
One matrix turns it into a real part.

63
00:05:03,205 --> 00:05:07,127
I scale it, then rotate it, then move it into place.

64
00:05:07,127 --> 00:05:11,367
The order matters, just as putting on socks and shoes does.

65
00:05:11,367 --> 00:05:22,607
Scale, rotate, translate puts a drone arm exactly where it belongs, but if I translate first and rotate afterwards, the arm swings away in a circle round the origin.

66
00:05:23,517 --> 00:05:27,157
Parts also hang on each other, like the bones of an arm.

67
00:05:27,157 --> 00:05:30,257
A rotor blade is placed relative to its hub.

68
00:05:30,257 --> 00:05:35,110
The hub is placed relative to the arm, and the arm relative to the body.

69
00:05:35,110 --> 00:05:39,743
The world matrix of the blade is the product of all of them, parent first.

70
00:05:39,743 --> 00:05:44,277
So when I turn the hub, the blade follows without a single extra line of code.

71
00:05:45,183 --> 00:05:48,700
The H key draws the local axes of these nodes.

72
00:05:48,700 --> 00:05:52,082
On a drone you can see the frames nested inside each other:

73
00:05:52,082 --> 00:05:55,839
the body, and inside it the two frames of the camera mount.

74
00:05:56,750 --> 00:05:59,507
The radar is a hierarchy in motion:

75
00:05:59,507 --> 00:06:03,614
the mount turns, and the dish tilts inside the frame that is turning.

76
00:06:04,517 --> 00:06:08,242
A soldier is the deepest hierarchy in the project:

77
00:06:08,242 --> 00:06:13,586
seventy-five parts on seven levels, from the hip to the thigh, the shin and the foot.

78
00:06:13,586 --> 00:06:16,757
When he walks, only the joint angles change.

79
00:06:17,650 --> 00:06:26,559
The camp now exists as numbers in three dimensions, but a screen is flat, so the next question is how a point in the world becomes a pixel.

80
00:06:26,559 --> 00:06:32,417
I describe the camera with three things, where the eye is, what it looks at, and which way is up.

81
00:06:32,417 --> 00:06:42,778
From these the program builds three axes, right, up and forward, and one matrix that moves the whole world so that the eye sits at the origin, looking straight down one axis.

82
00:06:43,683 --> 00:06:45,178
Then comes projection.

83
00:06:45,178 --> 00:06:48,058
A point twice as far away should look half as big.

84
00:06:48,058 --> 00:06:51,097
So x and y are divided by the distance.

85
00:06:51,097 --> 00:06:57,568
The pyramid of space that the camera sees is squeezed into a cube that runs from minus one to plus one.

86
00:06:57,568 --> 00:07:07,923
This table follows one real corner of a drone arm through every step, from the world to the view, to clip space, through the divide, and finally to its pixel.

87
00:07:08,817 --> 00:07:13,841
In the program, Control P changes the projection while everything keeps running.

88
00:07:13,841 --> 00:07:17,577
This is perspective, the way an eye or a camera sees.

89
00:07:18,483 --> 00:07:25,628
Orthographic from the top gives a map, because without the division by distance nothing gets smaller as it moves away.

90
00:07:25,628 --> 00:07:30,468
Here is the same camp from the front, like an engineer's elevation drawing.

91
00:07:30,468 --> 00:07:34,255
And here it is from the side, at exactly the same scale.

92
00:07:34,255 --> 00:07:39,507
Finally, this is the isometric view, in which all three axes are drawn equally.

93
00:07:40,417 --> 00:07:43,027
Not everything is in front of the camera.

94
00:07:43,027 --> 00:07:45,662
A line that leaves the window has to be cut.

95
00:07:45,662 --> 00:07:54,081
Cohen and Sutherland give each end of the line a four-bit code that says whether it is above, below, left or right of the window.

96
00:07:54,081 --> 00:07:58,248
If both codes are zero, the whole line is inside and I keep it.

97
00:07:58,248 --> 00:08:03,321
If the two codes share a bit, both ends are off the same side, and I throw the line away.

98
00:08:03,321 --> 00:08:13,057
For everything else, Liang and Barsky treat the line as a parameter running from zero to one, and each edge of the window narrows the part that survives.

99
00:08:13,950 --> 00:08:15,653
Lines are the easy case.

100
00:08:15,653 --> 00:08:27,185
A triangle that crosses the border is clipped by the method of Sutherland and Hodgman, one border at a time, keeping the corners that are inside and adding a new corner wherever a side crosses the border.

101
00:08:27,185 --> 00:08:36,990
And before any of that, I test a sphere around each whole object against the six planes of the view, so that an object behind the camera is never sent to the graphics card at all.

102
00:08:37,883 --> 00:08:42,344
And when two surfaces cover the same pixel, which one do you see?

103
00:08:42,344 --> 00:08:48,484
The depth buffer keeps, for every pixel, the distance of the nearest surface drawn so far.

104
00:08:48,484 --> 00:08:53,939
A new fragment is kept only if it is nearer, so the order of drawing no longer matters.

105
00:08:54,850 --> 00:08:57,914
With the Z key I switch the depth test off.

106
00:08:57,914 --> 00:09:03,085
Now whatever is drawn last simply wins, and the bunker shows through the ground.

107
00:09:03,085 --> 00:09:05,698
When I switch it on again, the scene is solid.

108
00:09:06,583 --> 00:09:13,279
And this is the depth buffer itself, drawn in grey, where white is near and black is far.

109
00:09:14,183 --> 00:09:19,367
A triangle now has three corners on the screen, so which pixels does it cover?

110
00:09:19,367 --> 00:09:25,212
For each edge I use a function that is positive on one side of the edge and negative on the other.

111
00:09:25,212 --> 00:09:29,734
A pixel is inside the triangle when all three functions are positive.

112
00:09:29,734 --> 00:09:37,247
And the same three numbers, scaled so that they add up to one, tell me how much of each corner's colour that pixel receives.

113
00:09:38,150 --> 00:09:42,648
That blending is colour interpolation, and it has one trap.

114
00:09:42,648 --> 00:09:47,549
If I blend along the screen, a texture on a receding surface bends.

115
00:09:47,549 --> 00:09:52,709
Dividing by depth first, and undoing it afterwards, keeps it straight.

116
00:09:52,709 --> 00:09:56,774
The workbook works the same triangle out by hand, pixel by pixel.

117
00:09:57,683 --> 00:10:00,833
The program can show all of this on its own frame.

118
00:10:00,833 --> 00:10:06,960
The back-quote key freezes one frame and replays how it was built, in sixteen stages.

119
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:10,355
Here the corners of every triangle are being transformed.

120
00:10:11,250 --> 00:10:16,578
Here the triangles are assembled, and then filled, one scan line after another.

121
00:10:19,250 --> 00:10:23,037
After that comes the depth test, which decides what is in front.

122
00:10:23,037 --> 00:10:25,917
Then the shadows are added from the light's own view.

123
00:10:25,917 --> 00:10:28,858
Then comes the fog, thicker with distance.

124
00:10:28,858 --> 00:10:32,596
Then the tone curve bends the brightness into the range of the screen.

125
00:10:32,596 --> 00:10:35,354
And at last, this is the finished frame.

126
00:10:36,250 --> 00:10:45,778
The explorer also has a pixel inspector and a small image laboratory, with blur and edge-detection kernels, a histogram, and a gamma control.

127
00:10:46,683 --> 00:10:51,413
Every pixel now knows which surface it shows, so what colour should it be?

128
00:10:51,413 --> 00:10:55,335
The point has a normal, the direction the surface is facing.

129
00:10:55,335 --> 00:10:59,171
There is a direction towards the light, and a direction towards the eye.

130
00:10:59,171 --> 00:11:05,004
Ambient light is a constant that stands in for all the light bouncing around the scene.

131
00:11:05,004 --> 00:11:12,896
Diffuse light depends on the angle between the normal and the light, which is why a face turned to the lamp is bright and a face turned away is dark.

132
00:11:12,896 --> 00:11:19,097
The highlight depends on how closely the normal lines up with the half-way vector between the light and the eye.

133
00:11:19,097 --> 00:11:24,171
Add the three, for every light, and you have the Phong illumination model.

134
00:11:25,083 --> 00:11:29,838
One rule I set for myself was that the lighting mathematics had to be my own.

135
00:11:29,838 --> 00:11:38,465
So the dot product, normalise, reflect, refract, mix and clamp are each written by hand and placed in front of every shader.

136
00:11:38,465 --> 00:11:42,243
The build even refuses a shader that calls the built-in version.

137
00:11:43,150 --> 00:11:46,814
Control L shows one term at a time in the running scene.

138
00:11:46,814 --> 00:11:48,670
This is ambient light alone.

139
00:11:49,583 --> 00:11:52,769
With diffuse light alone, the shapes appear.

140
00:11:52,769 --> 00:11:56,115
With specular light alone, only the highlights remain.

141
00:11:56,115 --> 00:11:57,959
And here are all three together.

142
00:11:59,583 --> 00:12:03,909
The camp has four kinds of light, and the keys one to four switch them.

143
00:12:03,909 --> 00:12:05,649
Key one switches the moon.

144
00:12:05,649 --> 00:12:08,897
Key two switches the red beacon on top of the mast.

145
00:12:08,897 --> 00:12:12,157
Key three switches the searchlights of the two drones.

146
00:12:12,157 --> 00:12:18,663
And key four switches the forty-three small lamps around the camp, each one a point light with its own range.

147
00:12:19,550 --> 00:12:22,994
The next question is where that light is computed.

148
00:12:22,994 --> 00:12:28,312
If I compute it once per triangle, I get flat shading, and you can see every facet.

149
00:12:28,312 --> 00:12:34,856
If I compute it at the corners and blend across, I get Gouraud shading, which is smooth but dulls the highlight.

150
00:12:34,856 --> 00:12:40,982
And if I blend the normal instead and compute the light at every pixel, I get Phong shading.

151
00:12:41,883 --> 00:12:44,971
On the helicopter, the O key steps through them.

152
00:12:44,971 --> 00:12:48,697
This is flat shading, where every facet shows.

153
00:12:48,697 --> 00:12:52,925
This is Gouraud shading, smooth, but with a dull highlight.

154
00:12:52,925 --> 00:12:57,459
And this is Phong shading, the one I use, because round parts should look round.

155
00:12:58,350 --> 00:13:03,227
A surface also needs detail, and I did not want to paint every wall by hand.

156
00:13:03,227 --> 00:13:05,041
So the patterns are procedural.

157
00:13:05,041 --> 00:13:12,970
I start with smooth random noise, add a second layer at twice the detail and half the strength, then a third and a fourth.

158
00:13:12,970 --> 00:13:22,110
The sum looks like concrete, sand or rust, and it is projected onto each part from the side its surface faces, so that it never stretches.

159
00:13:23,017 --> 00:13:26,706
The same pattern can also bend the normal instead of the colour.

160
00:13:26,706 --> 00:13:32,137
This is the same wall, without and with that relief, and not one triangle has been added.

161
00:13:33,050 --> 00:13:38,859
A mirror needs something that the rasteriser does not have, namely what lies behind the camera.

162
00:13:38,859 --> 00:13:42,057
Ray tracing gets it by following the light backwards.

163
00:13:42,057 --> 00:13:44,925
I send a ray from the eye through a pixel.

164
00:13:44,925 --> 00:13:54,079
Where it hits a surface, I send one ray to the light to test for shadow, one reflected ray, and through glass one refracted ray.

165
00:13:54,079 --> 00:14:07,898
Each of those can spawn more, up to five bounces, and because every part of the camp is a sphere, a box, a cylinder or a cone, each hit is solved exactly, while a tree of bounding boxes lets a ray skip everything it cannot reach.

166
00:14:08,783 --> 00:14:12,031
The semicolon key changes how the frame is made.

167
00:14:12,031 --> 00:14:14,279
This is the ordinary rasteriser.

168
00:14:15,183 --> 00:14:19,877
The hybrid mode keeps the rasteriser but traces the reflections.

169
00:14:19,877 --> 00:14:23,198
Whitted ray tracing adds refraction in the glass.

170
00:14:23,198 --> 00:14:28,136
Path tracing lets light bounce between surfaces, so shadows turn soft.

171
00:14:28,136 --> 00:14:31,575
And light tracing starts from the lamps instead of the eye.

172
00:14:32,483 --> 00:14:38,195
And with the ray debugger, a single click draws the whole tree of rays behind one pixel.

173
00:14:41,483 --> 00:14:42,966
That completes the lab.

174
00:14:42,966 --> 00:14:45,221
Everything from here on goes beyond it.

175
00:14:45,221 --> 00:14:52,187
I begin with the techniques that make the picture more believable, and then turn to the algorithms that make the world move.

176
00:14:53,083 --> 00:14:55,951
The highlight of the lab is a simple formula.

177
00:14:55,951 --> 00:15:02,201
Real surfaces are rough at a scale too small to see, so I added a microfacet model.

178
00:15:02,201 --> 00:15:09,517
It treats a surface as millions of tiny mirrors and asks how many of them happen to face the right way.

179
00:15:09,517 --> 00:15:15,979
After that, a material needs only two numbers, how rough it is and how metallic it is.

180
00:15:16,883 --> 00:15:19,885
The B key switches between the two on the helicopter.

181
00:15:19,885 --> 00:15:27,827
This is the classic model, and this is the microfacet model, where painted metal finally looks like painted metal.

182
00:15:28,717 --> 00:15:32,406
A shadow is really a question, can this point see the light?

183
00:15:32,406 --> 00:15:36,756
I first draw the scene from the position of the light and keep only the depth.

184
00:15:36,756 --> 00:15:43,987
Then each pixel compares its own distance to the light with the stored one, and if it is farther, something is in the way.

185
00:15:43,987 --> 00:15:55,429
To soften the edge I average many nearby tests, over a wider area where the shadow falls farther from the object that casts it, just as a real shadow blurs with distance.

186
00:15:56,317 --> 00:15:59,087
Here I switch the shadows off, and back on.

187
00:15:59,087 --> 00:16:07,045
And this is ambient occlusion, the soft darkening in corners where light struggles to get in, first off, and then on again.

188
00:16:07,950 --> 00:16:11,075
Light in a real scene has an enormous range.

189
00:16:11,075 --> 00:16:13,955
The sun is thousands of times brighter than a lamp.

190
00:16:13,955 --> 00:16:25,518
So I keep the true values while lighting, choose an exposure the way an eye adapts to a dark room, and only at the very end bend the result through a tone curve and gamma into the range that a screen can show.

191
00:16:26,417 --> 00:16:27,961
Now the air itself.

192
00:16:27,961 --> 00:16:31,527
Here I switch the fog off for a moment, and then bring it back.

193
00:16:31,527 --> 00:16:36,846
This is the glow around bright lights, which I also switch off for a moment, and bring back.

194
00:16:36,846 --> 00:16:41,369
And the N key returns to my very first renderer, which had none of these.

195
00:16:42,283 --> 00:16:43,974
Edges need one more idea.

196
00:16:43,974 --> 00:16:53,631
A pixel that tests only its centre turns a slanted edge into a staircase, and a thin wire flickers as it slips between two pixels.

197
00:16:53,631 --> 00:17:04,685
So in every frame the whole picture is shifted by a different fraction of a pixel, in a fixed pattern of eight positions, and each new frame is blended with the ones before it.

198
00:17:04,685 --> 00:17:11,275
After eight frames every pixel has looked at eight places inside itself, and the edge comes out smooth.

199
00:17:12,183 --> 00:17:15,712
Reflections in the rasteriser use a trick of their own.

200
00:17:15,712 --> 00:17:22,085
For a shiny pixel I march the reflected ray through the depth buffer and copy the colour where it lands.

201
00:17:22,085 --> 00:17:29,413
That is fast, but it can only reflect what is already on the screen, which is why the ray-traced modes are kept one key away.

202
00:17:29,413 --> 00:17:34,023
On the left is the screen-space result, and on the right the traced one.

203
00:17:34,917 --> 00:17:39,415
The ground is covered by four hundred and fifty thousand blades of grass.

204
00:17:39,415 --> 00:17:43,508
Sent one by one, they would bring any computer to its knees.

205
00:17:43,508 --> 00:17:54,255
So a single blade, with seven corners and five triangles, is sent to the graphics card once, together with a list of the places where it should be drawn, and that is called instancing.

206
00:17:54,255 --> 00:18:04,427
The trees use a second trick called level of detail, where a tree near the camera is a full mesh, a farther one is simpler, and the farthest are only flat pictures.

207
00:18:04,427 --> 00:18:10,965
The trees, by the way, are the only imported models in the project, and they are free public-domain meshes.

208
00:18:11,850 --> 00:18:15,392
Down in the grass, every blade bends in the wind.

209
00:18:15,392 --> 00:18:18,345
The F5 key steps through the quality presets.

210
00:18:18,345 --> 00:18:20,710
This is low, which runs on a laptop.

211
00:18:20,710 --> 00:18:21,715
This is high.

212
00:18:21,715 --> 00:18:23,076
This is ultra.

213
00:18:23,076 --> 00:18:26,898
And this is epic, the preset used throughout this video.

214
00:18:27,783 --> 00:18:34,215
I measured each preset on the same scene and the same machine, and this is the time that one frame costs.

215
00:18:35,117 --> 00:18:38,242
A picture becomes a world when time passes.

216
00:18:38,242 --> 00:18:43,536
The outpost stands at twenty-two point eight degrees north, the latitude of Khulna.

217
00:18:43,536 --> 00:18:47,691
From the hour, the program computes where the sun stands in the sky.

218
00:18:47,691 --> 00:18:55,705
A low sun shines through a longer path of air, so its light turns orange and weaker, as it does at every sunset.

219
00:18:55,705 --> 00:19:03,077
That one number, the height of the sun, then drives the colour of the light, the sky, the lamps and the exposure.

220
00:19:03,983 --> 00:19:07,427
Here is one whole day in twenty seconds.

221
00:19:07,427 --> 00:19:10,674
Dawn comes at about six, and the day shift walks out.

222
00:19:10,674 --> 00:19:14,020
Then noon, and the long light of the afternoon.

223
00:19:14,020 --> 00:19:18,479
At dusk the lamps come on, the drones lift off, and night falls.

224
00:19:24,983 --> 00:19:29,775
The R key brings rain, with falling drops and darker, shinier ground.

225
00:19:29,775 --> 00:19:34,247
And at night, the J key switches the fireflies and the dust in the air.

226
00:19:37,983 --> 00:19:43,204
Sound follows physics too, and every sound is synthesised from a simple recipe.

227
00:19:43,204 --> 00:19:48,951
A cannon shot is a burst of noise pushed through a filter, with a low thump underneath.

228
00:19:48,951 --> 00:19:53,118
A rotor is a stack of tones at the rate at which the blades pass.

229
00:19:53,118 --> 00:19:59,932
A distant sound arrives late, because sound is slow, and dull, because air swallows the high tones first.

230
00:19:59,932 --> 00:20:03,142
A loud shot echoes off the wall and the treeline.

231
00:20:03,142 --> 00:20:07,311
And inside the bunker, the same shot rings in a concrete room.

232
00:20:08,217 --> 00:20:10,791
So far I have only been looking at the outpost.

233
00:20:10,791 --> 00:20:13,180
But a world you can only look at is still a model.

234
00:20:13,180 --> 00:20:16,089
This one can be walked through, driven and flown.

235
00:20:16,983 --> 00:20:27,155
Under the soldiers there is a second, invisible map, made of the floors they may stand on, the walls that stop them, and the doorways that join one room to the next.

236
00:20:27,155 --> 00:20:32,079
A door is a gate in that map, and it opens when a soldier comes close and faces it.

237
00:20:32,983 --> 00:20:38,431
Here I am controlling a soldier as he walks down the ramp, and the blast gate opens for him.

238
00:20:39,983 --> 00:20:43,535
We go through the tunnel and into the command room under the ground.

239
00:20:46,483 --> 00:20:51,811
Other doors are opened by hand with the E key, like this one to the generator room.

240
00:20:52,717 --> 00:20:57,277
And this is the barracks hall, where the soldiers who are off duty rest and train.

241
00:21:00,217 --> 00:21:06,026
Outdoors, a soldier walks with W, A, S and D, and sprints with Shift.

242
00:21:06,026 --> 00:21:10,842
The V key puts me behind his eyes in first person, and takes me back out again.

243
00:21:10,842 --> 00:21:15,097
He can also jump, crouch, aim and fire his rifle.

244
00:21:15,983 --> 00:21:18,655
Twenty things in the camp can be operated.

245
00:21:18,655 --> 00:21:22,405
Here they are, and the rule is the same for every one of them.

246
00:21:22,405 --> 00:21:30,297
I select it, take control of it, and then its own keys work, for driving, aiming, firing and one special action.

247
00:21:30,297 --> 00:21:37,271
The V key changes the camera, from outside to the gunner's sight, and Escape hands the thing back to itself.

248
00:21:38,183 --> 00:21:39,617
Let us start with the tank.

249
00:21:39,617 --> 00:21:42,311
The soldier walks up to it and climbs in with E.

250
00:21:43,683 --> 00:21:48,675
He drives it out of the bay, turns on the spot, and swings the turret onto the target.

251
00:21:52,183 --> 00:21:55,399
And from the gunner's sight, he fires two rounds.

252
00:21:59,583 --> 00:22:03,831
The anti-aircraft gun has six barrels and fires bursts of tracer.

253
00:22:04,717 --> 00:22:07,597
And this is the surface-to-air missile launcher.

254
00:22:09,117 --> 00:22:11,568
The number keys jump straight to a unit.

255
00:22:11,568 --> 00:22:17,373
Eight takes the radar, and the arrow keys swing its dish across the sector it scans.

256
00:22:18,283 --> 00:22:20,550
Even the small things can be operated.

257
00:22:20,550 --> 00:22:23,467
This is the barrier at the gate, raised with Enter.

258
00:22:25,283 --> 00:22:30,467
And nine takes the blast doors of the bunker, which close in about four seconds.

259
00:22:32,783 --> 00:22:34,241
Now the helicopter.

260
00:22:34,241 --> 00:22:41,423
Its special action starts the engine, the rotor spools up, and it lifts off, turns, and flies out over the fence.

261
00:22:50,283 --> 00:22:56,692
A drone can be flown off its pad by hand as well, and the V key steps through its three cameras.

262
00:22:56,692 --> 00:22:58,420
This is the view from behind it.

263
00:22:58,420 --> 00:23:00,491
This is the view from its nose.

264
00:23:00,491 --> 00:23:02,715
And this is the view through its thermal camera.

265
00:23:07,283 --> 00:23:11,205
I can even change sides and drive one of the intruders myself.

266
00:23:11,205 --> 00:23:16,211
When I press Backspace, it goes back to its own planner and carries on with its mission.

267
00:23:17,283 --> 00:23:26,401
Every moving thing also has a point of view, each soldier, each vehicle, both drones, every intruder, and even a round in flight.

268
00:23:26,401 --> 00:23:32,528
To choose one I click on it, and the program has to work out what a flat click means in a world with depth.

269
00:23:32,528 --> 00:23:40,923
It runs the camera's mathematics backwards and turns the pixel into a ray, and the first object that the ray hits is the one I get.

270
00:23:41,817 --> 00:23:44,035
A soldier is walking his patrol.

271
00:23:44,035 --> 00:23:46,521
I move the pointer onto him, and click.

272
00:23:48,817 --> 00:23:50,349
Now I see what he sees.

273
00:23:50,349 --> 00:23:54,985
He keeps walking his patrol, and I simply ride along in first person.

274
00:23:56,317 --> 00:24:00,157
The V key steps back behind him, into third person.

275
00:24:02,317 --> 00:24:07,909
F4 lists everything that moves, with the nearest first, and I can click any row.

276
00:24:10,317 --> 00:24:13,087
The same jump works for anything that moves.

277
00:24:13,087 --> 00:24:15,574
This is one of the tanks, seen from behind.

278
00:24:15,574 --> 00:24:17,560
This is the helicopter on its pad.

279
00:24:17,560 --> 00:24:20,085
And this is a drone, waiting for the night.

280
00:24:20,983 --> 00:24:22,821
At night it becomes a game.

281
00:24:22,821 --> 00:24:27,637
The K key hands the camera to a director that cuts between these views by itself.

282
00:24:27,637 --> 00:24:30,847
Here it opens the list and goes into a drone's camera.

283
00:24:31,750 --> 00:24:35,782
A round is launched, and we ride it all the way down to the target.

284
00:24:38,550 --> 00:24:43,158
Or we take the other side, inside an intruder while the drones hunt it.

285
00:24:44,350 --> 00:24:47,734
Or we stand with a sentry at the gate, behind his rifle.

286
00:24:50,750 --> 00:24:53,029
Nobody controls the soldiers.

287
00:24:53,029 --> 00:24:58,887
Each has a duty for the day shift and another for the night, and has to walk there by himself.

288
00:24:58,887 --> 00:25:10,640
He searches a grid with the A* algorithm, which always explores the most promising cell first, then pulls the zigzag path straight like a string, and finally rounds its corners into a smooth curve.

289
00:25:10,640 --> 00:25:15,350
On that curve he slows down wherever the bend is tight, as a person would.

290
00:25:16,250 --> 00:25:20,429
His legs are solved backwards, which is called inverse kinematics.

291
00:25:20,429 --> 00:25:25,502
The planted foot stays exactly where it is on the ground, so it never slides.

292
00:25:25,502 --> 00:25:34,939
The hip, the knee and the foot form a triangle whose two sides, the thigh and the shin, have fixed lengths, so the knee angle follows from the law of cosines.

293
00:25:34,939 --> 00:25:38,090
Meanwhile the other foot swings forward in an arc.

294
00:25:38,983 --> 00:25:41,409
By day each soldier has work of his own.

295
00:25:41,409 --> 00:25:45,221
This one walks through the bunker to his console in the command room.

296
00:25:45,221 --> 00:25:48,247
And outside, the sentries stand guard at the gate.

297
00:25:51,983 --> 00:25:55,157
When the camp goes on alert, every weapon is manned.

298
00:25:55,157 --> 00:25:57,815
The missile canisters rise through the net.

299
00:25:58,717 --> 00:26:04,405
The rocket truck raises its pod to firing elevation, and the gun searches its sector.

300
00:26:05,317 --> 00:26:09,085
And the tanks wait in their bays with their guns over the wall.

301
00:26:09,983 --> 00:26:13,427
One more piece of engineering sits under all of this.

302
00:26:13,427 --> 00:26:18,059
The world does not advance by whatever time a frame happened to take.

303
00:26:18,059 --> 00:26:24,897
It advances in fixed steps of one sixtieth of a second, and the picture is blended between the last two steps.

304
00:26:24,897 --> 00:26:37,439
So the motion stays smooth at any frame rate, and the same seed always gives exactly the same night, which is what lets me record a whole play session with F9 and replay it later, event for event.

305
00:26:38,350 --> 00:26:40,372
Now to the algorithms.

306
00:26:40,372 --> 00:26:44,171
The drones have to find an intruder in the dark without any help from me.

307
00:26:44,171 --> 00:26:49,870
So what does a machine actually see, and how does it decide that a warm spot is a target?

308
00:26:50,783 --> 00:26:53,209
A thermal camera does not see light.

309
00:26:53,209 --> 00:26:54,361
It sees heat.

310
00:26:54,361 --> 00:27:03,614
So every part in the camp is given a temperature, where an engine is hot, a soldier is warm, and the ground is cool and slowly warmed by the sun.

311
00:27:03,614 --> 00:27:09,227
A rover even leaves warm tyre tracks behind it, which fade as the ground cools.

312
00:27:09,227 --> 00:27:25,151
A real sensor is far from perfect, and I model that as well, so the lens blurs the picture, every pixel has its own small error, whole columns show up as stripes, there is noise, and from time to time a shutter closes so that the camera can calibrate itself again.

313
00:27:26,050 --> 00:27:33,082
The F key switches the whole view to thermal, where hot is bright, and the T key changes the palette.

314
00:27:35,050 --> 00:27:42,624
And this is what a drone's own camera sends down, three hundred and twenty by two hundred and forty pixels, with all of its noise.

315
00:27:42,624 --> 00:27:48,506
The Q key makes the sensor calibrate itself, and for a moment its shutter closes.

316
00:27:48,506 --> 00:27:51,582
The mouse wheel zooms the lens in three steps.

317
00:27:51,582 --> 00:27:54,094
This is the wide view of forty degrees.

318
00:27:54,094 --> 00:27:55,712
This is twenty degrees.

319
00:27:55,712 --> 00:27:59,362
And this is eight degrees, for a close look at a target.

320
00:28:00,250 --> 00:28:04,196
Every frame of that picture goes through the same five steps.

321
00:28:04,196 --> 00:28:14,882
First comes a threshold, which keeps only the pixels above eighty per cent heat, or above the level that best separates the dark part of the picture from the bright part, whichever is higher.

322
00:28:14,882 --> 00:28:20,348
Second, single stray pixels are removed, because noise is never a vehicle.

323
00:28:20,348 --> 00:28:24,270
Third, neighbouring hot pixels are joined into regions.

324
00:28:24,270 --> 00:28:26,574
Fourth, each region is measured.

325
00:28:26,574 --> 00:28:29,506
And fifth, the program decides what it is.

326
00:28:30,417 --> 00:28:33,321
That automatic level deserves a closer look.

327
00:28:33,321 --> 00:28:43,027
If I count how many pixels there are of each brightness, a night picture shows two hills, a large one for the cold background and a small one for the warm things.

328
00:28:43,027 --> 00:28:51,189
Otsu's method tries every possible cut between them and keeps the one that leaves each side as tightly packed as possible.

329
00:28:51,189 --> 00:28:55,737
So the threshold adapts by itself when the night grows warmer or colder.

330
00:28:56,650 --> 00:29:03,390
Measuring a region gives its area, its centre, its hottest pixel, and how stretched it is.

331
00:29:03,390 --> 00:29:05,363
Then a simple rule decides.

332
00:29:05,363 --> 00:29:11,380
A small region with a very hot core that is not too stretched is a vehicle, and therefore a target.

333
00:29:11,380 --> 00:29:18,513
A warm region without a hot core, upright or compact, is a person, and a person is never fired at.

334
00:29:18,513 --> 00:29:22,906
Anything else, like a wall warmed by the sun, is clutter.

335
00:29:23,817 --> 00:29:25,815
By day I can check this rule.

336
00:29:25,815 --> 00:29:31,977
Through the drone's camera the soldiers are boxed as persons, and not one of them ever becomes a target.

337
00:29:32,883 --> 00:29:39,709
One detection is not enough, because the camera shakes, pixels flicker, and a rover drives behind a tree.

338
00:29:39,709 --> 00:29:47,025
So each target becomes a track, which is a guess of its position and speed, together with how unsure that guess is.

339
00:29:47,025 --> 00:29:54,758
Between frames the track is moved ahead by its speed, and the uncertainty grows, like walking with your eyes closed.

340
00:29:54,758 --> 00:30:04,770
When a new detection arrives, it pulls the guess back, strongly if the track was unsure and only gently if it was confident, and the uncertainty shrinks again.

341
00:30:04,770 --> 00:30:08,619
This cycle of predict and correct is the Kalman filter.

342
00:30:09,517 --> 00:30:15,804
With several targets there is also a matching problem, namely which detection belongs to which track?

343
00:30:15,804 --> 00:30:23,806
Only a detection inside a track's gate may match it, and among those the Hungarian method picks the pairing with the smallest total distance.

344
00:30:23,806 --> 00:30:32,413
And when both drones see the same target, their two lines of sight are crossed, which fixes its position far better than a single camera can.

345
00:30:33,317 --> 00:30:36,099
In the drone's view you can watch this happen.

346
00:30:36,099 --> 00:30:37,631
The boxes are tracks.

347
00:30:37,631 --> 00:30:44,621
When the rover goes behind cover, its track coasts on the prediction alone, and it is picked up again when the rover comes out.

348
00:30:45,517 --> 00:30:51,767
Here is that same night as numbers, the uncertainty of one track over time.

349
00:30:51,767 --> 00:30:56,461
It jumps while the rover is hidden, and falls back each time a camera sees it again.

350
00:30:57,350 --> 00:30:59,482
Seeing is only half of it.

351
00:30:59,482 --> 00:31:05,426
Three kinds of things in the camp act on their own, the drones, the intruders and the soldiers.

352
00:31:05,426 --> 00:31:09,134
So let us look at the rules that make them behave as if they had a plan.

353
00:31:10,050 --> 00:31:13,040
A drone has a duty for each part of the day.

354
00:31:13,040 --> 00:31:14,989
By day it sits docked on its pad.

355
00:31:14,989 --> 00:31:17,893
At dusk its rotors spool up and it takes off.

356
00:31:17,893 --> 00:31:24,658
At night the two of them patrol on opposite sides of a circle, so that together they always see the whole camp.

357
00:31:24,658 --> 00:31:28,650
And at dawn they return, hover over their pads, and land.

358
00:31:29,550 --> 00:31:31,278
Here is dusk.

359
00:31:31,278 --> 00:31:35,454
The lamps come on, and both drones lift off with their searchlights.

360
00:31:36,550 --> 00:31:40,654
And here they are on patrol, with their searchlights sweeping the camp.

361
00:31:41,550 --> 00:31:52,739
When a track is confirmed, the nearer drone becomes the lead and the other becomes its wing, and they take up positions about ninety degrees apart, so that one of them almost always has a clear view.

362
00:31:52,739 --> 00:32:01,574
Before firing, the lead must hold its lock for eight tenths of a second and the wing for half a second, each with nothing in its line of fire.

363
00:32:01,574 --> 00:32:07,782
If there is only one intruder, the lead waits up to eight seconds for the wing, so that both can fire.

364
00:32:08,683 --> 00:32:10,815
They do not fire at the same moment.

365
00:32:10,815 --> 00:32:18,659
The round from the farther drone needs longer to arrive, so that drone fires first, by exactly the difference in flight time.

366
00:32:18,659 --> 00:32:26,342
Both rounds then arrive together, which is called time on target, and it leaves a rover no chance to escape between two hits.

367
00:32:26,342 --> 00:32:34,363
Each round also steers itself on the way, turning towards its target at a limited rate, and goes off when it comes within half a metre.

368
00:32:35,250 --> 00:32:36,292
Here it is.

369
00:32:36,292 --> 00:32:41,874
One intruder is left, both drones hold their locks, and both rounds arrive together.

370
00:32:43,750 --> 00:32:45,956
Now a second intruder appears.

371
00:32:45,956 --> 00:32:53,946
If it is more than fourteen metres from the first, and it is close to something it could damage, then waiting for a combined strike would cost too much.

372
00:32:53,946 --> 00:32:55,343
So the pair splits.

373
00:32:55,343 --> 00:32:58,897
The nearer drone takes the second target and strikes alone.

374
00:32:58,897 --> 00:33:05,110
When that target is gone, or the two targets come within nine metres of each other, the pair forms again.

375
00:33:06,017 --> 00:33:08,505
This is the same night, a little earlier.

376
00:33:08,505 --> 00:33:13,817
Two intruders are far apart, the pair splits, and one drone fires alone.

377
00:33:14,717 --> 00:33:16,825
The guards join in as well.

378
00:33:16,825 --> 00:33:22,541
A soldier at his post opens fire on any tracked intruder within twenty-six metres.

379
00:33:23,450 --> 00:33:25,251
Now let us change sides.

380
00:33:25,251 --> 00:33:36,073
An intruder is a small rover that wants to reach something in the camp without being seen, and to plan its way it lays a grid over the ground, eighty by eighty cells of half a metre each.

381
00:33:36,073 --> 00:33:38,282
Cells inside obstacles are blocked.

382
00:33:39,183 --> 00:33:41,818
Every free cell then gets a price.

383
00:33:41,818 --> 00:33:43,730
It costs one just to step on it.

384
00:33:43,730 --> 00:33:52,357
It costs more if a drone can see it, and to find that out, the rover draws a line from each drone to the cell and checks whether anything is in the way.

385
00:33:52,357 --> 00:33:58,599
And each rover adds a little random cost of its own, so that no two rovers choose the same way.

386
00:33:59,483 --> 00:34:03,159
Then comes a trick from the MicroMouse maze-solving robots.

387
00:34:03,159 --> 00:34:11,517
I start at the goal with zero and let the numbers flood outwards like water, each cell taking its cheapest neighbour plus its own price.

388
00:34:11,517 --> 00:34:17,155
When the flood reaches the rover, every cell knows how expensive the rest of the journey is.

389
00:34:17,155 --> 00:34:23,915
So the route needs no search at all, because the rover simply steps to the neighbour with the smaller number, again and again.

390
00:34:24,817 --> 00:34:32,953
The M key shows this inside the program, with the flood field as coloured cells and the route as a line, and each rover has its own.

391
00:34:33,850 --> 00:34:36,129
A route is not yet a movement.

392
00:34:36,129 --> 00:34:41,803
The rover follows it the way a line-follower robot follows a black line on the floor.

393
00:34:41,803 --> 00:34:46,546
Five sensors across its nose each report whether they are over the line.

394
00:34:46,546 --> 00:34:52,441
Their weighted average is the error, which tells the rover how far the line lies to one side.

395
00:34:52,441 --> 00:35:06,130
A controller then turns that error, together with how fast it is changing, into a turn rate, much as a driver watches both where the lane is and how quickly the car is drifting, and the two wheels are driven at different speeds.

396
00:35:07,017 --> 00:35:12,912
Here is one of them from above, shown in thermal so that it stands out against the cold ground.

397
00:35:12,912 --> 00:35:20,721
It weaves a little, changes its speed, and sometimes stops, so that a tracker cannot simply extend a straight line.

398
00:35:21,617 --> 00:35:24,281
And this is the view from the camera on its own mast.

399
00:35:27,617 --> 00:35:29,161
And the rover listens.

400
00:35:29,161 --> 00:35:32,997
When a drone comes within five metres, it drops what it was doing.

401
00:35:32,997 --> 00:35:35,166
Its goal is no longer the camp.

402
00:35:35,166 --> 00:35:42,102
Its goal becomes every cell that no drone can see, under a tree, behind a wall, or under a net.

403
00:35:42,102 --> 00:35:48,720
The same flood fill runs again, this time from all of those cells at once, and the rover drives to the nearest one.

404
00:35:48,720 --> 00:35:53,681
There it waits four seconds and then plans again, because by then the drones have moved.

405
00:35:54,583 --> 00:35:57,941
Here are three views of the same few seconds.

406
00:35:57,941 --> 00:36:00,809
On the left is the drone's thermal camera.

407
00:36:00,809 --> 00:36:07,965
In the middle is the rover's own map, red where a drone can see, dark where it is hidden, with its route in yellow.

408
00:36:07,965 --> 00:36:09,951
On the right is the wide view.

409
00:36:09,951 --> 00:36:14,239
Watch the route bend into the shadow as the drone comes near.

410
00:36:15,150 --> 00:36:18,937
Put together, all of this becomes a game called Night Watch.

411
00:36:18,937 --> 00:36:22,822
Rovers come in waves, two more each time, up to six.

412
00:36:22,822 --> 00:36:26,486
Each has one hundred points of armour, and a hit takes about thirty.

413
00:36:26,486 --> 00:36:30,689
A kill scores one hundred points, and a combined kill one hundred and fifty.

414
00:36:30,689 --> 00:36:34,206
A rover that reaches its goal costs the base ten per cent.

415
00:36:35,117 --> 00:36:45,191
The game's display shows the wave and the score at the top, the health of the base as a green bar, the clock, and a small radar on which every tracked intruder appears.

416
00:36:45,191 --> 00:36:50,093
And with the U key, the picture from a drone's thermal camera is added in the corner.

417
00:36:50,983 --> 00:36:54,733
With Shift and F2 the same game runs in daylight.

418
00:36:54,733 --> 00:36:59,767
The intruders come by day, and the drones scramble from their pads to meet them.

419
00:37:00,983 --> 00:37:03,630
The blast itself is physics too.

420
00:37:03,630 --> 00:37:07,245
Hundreds of particles are thrown out with random speeds.

421
00:37:07,245 --> 00:37:12,392
Gravity pulls them down, drag slows them, and they bounce once on the ground.

422
00:37:12,392 --> 00:37:20,903
Each one cools as it flies, and its colour follows its temperature, from white through yellow, orange and red to dark smoke.

423
00:37:21,817 --> 00:37:26,281
Afterwards the wreck burns, and the fire slowly cools.

424
00:37:27,817 --> 00:37:32,089
At dawn the rovers that are left withdraw, and the drones come home.

425
00:37:34,817 --> 00:37:39,057
It did not all work the first time, and four problems took me the longest.

426
00:37:39,057 --> 00:37:44,756
Far surfaces flickered, because depth is stored most precisely close to the camera.

427
00:37:44,756 --> 00:37:49,069
Shadows had stripes, because a surface was shadowing itself.

428
00:37:49,069 --> 00:37:52,440
Thin wires shimmered from one frame to the next.

429
00:37:52,440 --> 00:37:57,121
And soldiers walked through walls, until the doors became part of the walk map.

430
00:37:57,121 --> 00:38:02,753
Each of these is in the report with its cause, its fix, and a picture from before and after.

431
00:38:03,650 --> 00:38:07,841
I also wanted every claim in this video to be checkable.

432
00:38:07,841 --> 00:38:11,824
Three hundred and ninety-six automatic tests run on every build.

433
00:38:11,824 --> 00:38:17,057
The same seed always gives exactly the same night, so any result can be repeated.

434
00:38:17,057 --> 00:38:20,330
And a whole play session can be recorded and replayed.

435
00:38:21,217 --> 00:38:23,398
And this brings me back to the workbook.

436
00:38:23,398 --> 00:38:32,283
Every coordinate of every part, every constant of every algorithm, and every number in the report lives there, on twenty-three sheets.

437
00:38:32,283 --> 00:38:37,681
A checker stops the build if the program, the workbook and the report ever disagree.

438
00:38:38,583 --> 00:38:43,167
To finish, here is one night at the outpost, with no more words from me.

439
00:39:02,583 --> 00:39:15,207
That was ThermalSense, from a single triangle to sixteen primitives, five thousand four hundred and seventy-four parts, a complete pipeline, a thermal camera, a tracker, and three kinds of agents.

440
00:39:16,117 --> 00:39:18,580
One last word, to my juniors.

441
00:39:18,580 --> 00:39:20,468
Be brave with your projects.

442
00:39:20,468 --> 00:39:24,291
Pick a problem that scares you a little, and try to solve it elegantly.

443
00:39:24,291 --> 00:39:34,156
Because I believe that if student A and student B both use AI for a project, what matters is how creative you can be, and how much depth you can cover.

444
00:39:34,156 --> 00:39:35,293
Thank you for watching.
